<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Drug-induced hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, resolved (E23.1)</content>. Testosterone 452 ng/dL with LH 4.8 and FSH 5.2 mIU/mL: the axis has recovered without treatment, about four months after the last estrogen injection. Hemoglobin 14.4 g/dL. [S]<list><item>No further hormone tests needed except testosterone and estradiol once more at six months.</item><item>Bone density scan not needed, as the hypogonadism lasted under six months.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Bilateral gynecomastia, improving (N62)</content>. Continuing to shrink. [S]<list><item>Continue watchful waiting; review at six months.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Major depressive disorder, single episode, responding (F32.1)</content>. PHQ-9 7, down 53 percent from 15: a response, not yet remission. Bupropion is well tolerated; blood pressure 124/78 mmHg. Suicide risk remains low. [S]<list><item>Continue bupropion XL 150 mg; psychotherapy to the end of the course of 16 sessions, then monthly.</item><item>Collaborative safety plan reviewed and unchanged; 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline and the clinic number confirmed.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Breast examination for size, tenderness, and any discrete mass</td><td>Every visit until the gynecomastia settles</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Glandular tissue about 2.5 cm on the left and 1.5 cm on the right, tender only on firm pressure; no discrete mass</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Testosterone, LH, and FSH</td><td>Every 6 to 8 weeks after the exposure stops, until normal</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Testosterone 452 ng/dL, LH 4.8 and FSH 5.2 mIU/mL: all normal; recovery complete</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Hemoglobin while testosterone is low</td><td>With each hormone check until normal</td><td>Completed today</td><td>14.4 g/dL</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Bone density (DXA)</td><td>If hypogonadism persists beyond six months or a fragility fracture occurs</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>The axis recovered within about four months of the last estrogen dose</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Testosterone therapy</td><td>Only for persistent, symptomatic, confirmed testosterone deficiency</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not given while the axis recovers: it would suppress LH and FSH again and is not indicated for a transient, drug-induced low testosterone</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PHQ-9</td><td>Baseline, then at least monthly during acute treatment; every 2 to 4 weeks in continuation</td><td>Completed today</td><td>7, down 53 percent from 15: response; remission not yet reached</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide risk reassessment (C-SSRS Since Last Visit and PHQ-9 item 9)</td><td>Every visit during active treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No suicidal thoughts, intent, plan, or behavior since the last visit; PHQ-9 item 9 scored 0. Safety plan reviewed</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Antidepressant tolerability check two weeks after starting</td><td>2 weeks after starting and after each dose change</td><td>Deferred</td><td>Not done, a deviation from the protocol: the nurse telephone check two weeks after bupropion was started, which was also to supply the monthly PHQ-9, was not completed after two unanswered calls while he was abroad, and no further call was booked, leaving eight weeks without a score. No harm resulted judging by today&apos;s review: the early insomnia and dry mouth settled, blood pressure is 124/78 mmHg, and there were no suicidal thoughts. Corrective action: a missed safety call is now rebooked within two days and flagged to Dr. Hallam if it cannot be completed</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Adherence, adverse effects, and blood pressure on bupropion</td><td>Every visit during active treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No missed doses; early dry mouth and insomnia settled; blood pressure 124/78 mmHg</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Psychotherapy, sleep, running, and social contact</td><td>Every visit during active treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Fourteen sessions; sleep about seven hours; running about 35 km a week</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Lithium, anticonvulsant, or antipsychotic monitoring (levels, metabolic tests, AIMS)</td><td>Only on those drugs</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not on any of these drugs</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Testosterone, LH, and FSH back in the reference range within six months of the last estrogen dose, without testosterone therapy. Outcome by 1953-08-04: Testosterone 452 ng/dL with normal LH and FSH at sixteen weeks [S]</item><item>Breast tenderness gone and gynecomastia reduced; a surgical opinion offered if it persists and troubles him. In progress; target 1953-10-06 [S]</item><item>Depression response (PHQ-9 down by at least 50 percent) by sixteen weeks, then remission (PHQ-9 below 5). In progress; target 1953-10-06 [S]</item><item>Back to running about 40 km a week and changing at his club again. In progress; target 1953-10-06 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: EAA clinical practice guidelines: gynecomastia evaluation and management (2019); Testosterone Therapy in Men With Hypogonadism: An Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline (2018); Osteoporosis in Men: An Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline (2012); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Major Depressive Disorder (2022); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Six-month review in two months with testosterone, estradiol, vitamin D, and PHQ-9. Next scheduled visit 1953-10-06: six-month review of gynecomastia and depression in continuation treatment. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
